15 0 obj
<</A 54 0 R/Alt(Various images of trees with green leaves and red or unripe green berries. Each image highlights different clusters of berries, demonstrating varying stages of ripeness and lighting conditions. The background includes blue sky and soil, indicating an outdoor environment.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 36 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

16 0 obj
<</A 55 0 R/Alt(Various photographs show a fruit-bearing tree with green leaves and red fruits in different lighting and perspective conditions. Each image from \(a\) to \(h\) varies in brightness, contrast, or filter effect, impacting the visibility of details and colors in the scene.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 37 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

17 0 obj
<</A 56 0 R/Alt(Diagram of an Efficient Hybrid Encoder for image processing. It includes convolutional layers, Multifaceted Efficient Modules \(MFEM\), and Advanced Inception with Fine-grained Information \(AIFI\). The flow shows image features through a series of fusions, CMKBlock, and IOU-aware query selection to the decoder and head. Insets display stages \(S1-S5\), position embedding, image feature, and object query components. Visual examples of red fruits assist in illustrating the concept.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 39 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

18 0 obj
<</A 57 0 R/Alt(Diagram showing a multi-stage process with four stages. Each stage consists of two blocks labeled \215Conv 3x3\216 and \215MFEM.\216 Input enters the first Conv 3x3 block in Stage 1 and progresses through all stages in sequence.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 41 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

19 0 obj
<</A 58 0 R/Alt(Flowchart illustrating a convolutional neural network module. The process begins with a \\\(1 \\times 1\\\) convolution, splits into two \\\(c/2\\\) partitions. One branch goes through a \\\(3 \\times 3\\\) Repconv, followed by two \\\(3 \\times 3\\\) convolutions. Both branches merge in a concatenation step, leading to a final \\\(1 \\times 1\\\) convolution. Inset diagrams show operations involving \\\(3 \\times 3\\\) and \\\(1 \\times 1\\\) convolutions. The key explains partition, convolution, SiLU, and identity operations.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 42 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

20 0 obj
<</A 59 0 R/Alt(Diagram with two parts labeled \(a\) and \(b\), each showing a flow of data: \(a\) displays three \215Fusion\216 blocks receiving inputs F5, S4, and S3, culminating in a circle labeled \215C\216. \(b\) also culminates in \215C\216 but includes an additional \215CMKBlock\216 and an initial \215SPDConv\216 block, with inputs F5, S4, S3, and S2.)/K 1/P 8 0 R/Pg 42 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

21 0 obj
<</A 60 0 R/Alt(Illustration of a computational process divided into five stages: \(a\) a 3D cube labeled with dimensions C1, S, and S; \(b\) the same cube in different colors; \(c\) four smaller stacks of cubes merging into a single entity labeled C; \(d\) a long rectangular block marked 4C1; \(e\) a smaller cube labeled C2 resulting from a convolution operation with a 3x3 filter, showing reduced dimensions S/2. Arrows indicate the progression from one stage to the next, highlighting a transformation process involving space-to-depth conversion followed by convolution.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 43 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

22 0 obj
<</A 61 0 R/Alt(Diagrams illustrating neural network architecture components, labeled \(a\) to \(d\). Panel \(a\) shows convolution and concatenation processes with MDCM. Panel \(b\) details a series of depth-wise convolutions. Panel \(c\) and \(d\) demonstrate transformations involving Fast Fourier Transform \(FFT\) and Inverse Fast Fourier Transform \(IFFT\), with Conv 1x1 operations. Legend indicates components like depth-wise convolution in green, FFT in pink, and IFFT in peach.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 44 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

23 0 obj
<</A 62 0 R/Alt(Flowchart depicting a neural network process. Inputs with dimensions C, H, W are processed through Conv2d, generating offsets with dimensions 2N, H, W. An algorithm calculates new coordinates using these offsets and original coordinates. Resampling adjusts dimensions to C, 6, H, W, followed by reshaping, convolution, normalization, and SiLU activation to produce the output.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 48 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

24 0 obj
<</A 63 0 R/Alt(Three rows of images compare fruit detection techniques on trees. Each row contains three columns labeled Original, RT-DERT, and MDE-DERT. Row \(a\) shows branches with various fruits highlighted in detail. Row \(b\) displays a tree section with fruits marked for analysis. Row \(c\) offers a broader perspective of a tree with numerous fruits identified and annotated for study.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 49 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

25 0 obj
<</A 64 0 R/Alt(A series of six images shows a tree with dense foliage and fruit. Image \(a\) features a natural view, while images \(b\) through \(f\) use color-coded overlays to indicate heat or energy distribution, transitioning from cooler blues to warmer reds, highlighting differences in detection or observation.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 50 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

26 0 obj
<</A 65 0 R/Alt(Scatter plot showing predicted values against ground truth, with green dots closely following a dashed trend line. Metrics: R-squared 0.9788, Mean Absolute Error 6.78, Root Mean Square Error 7.75.)/K 0/P 8 0 R/Pg 51 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

27 0 obj
<</A 66 0 R/Alt(Images showing bayberry plants under RT-DERT and MDE-DERT detection. Each set consists of five photos with detected bayberry counts: \(a\) RT-DERT 31, MDE-DERT 35; \(b\) RT-DERT 94, MDE-DERT 104; \(c\) RT-DERT 60, MDE-DERT 67; \(d\) RT-DERT 134, MDE-DERT 162; \(e\) RT-DERT 127, MDE-DERT 160. Second set: \(f\) RT-DERT 61, MDE-DERT 59; \(g\) RT-DERT 49, MDE-DERT 53; \(h\) RT-DERT 47, MDE-DERT 51; \(i\) RT-DERT 79, MDE-DERT 90; \(j\) RT-DERT 141, MDE-DERT 162. Each image shows varying amounts of bayberries on the plants.)/K 1/P 8 0 R/Pg 39 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>
endobj

